From 09d933ef85fde3b7f3ff5182428579ec4d85347e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:58:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2251/2271] drm/rockchip: vop2: Plan the video PLL for all
 video ports in atomic_check

All RK3588 video ports can take their DCLK from the video PLL (V0PLL),
but atomic_check decides about it for each port on its own, so it may
accept a state that the commit then runs at a pixel clock it refused to
accept, or refuse one depending on what ran before:

- The closest CRU rate of a port is rounded with the PLL at its current
  rate. When a port checked earlier retunes the PLL in the same commit,
  that rate is gone by the time the port is enabled. From all outputs
  off, with V0PLL left at 50.35 MHz by an earlier 640x480 DisplayPort
  mode, HDMI 4096x2160@59.94Hz 10 bpc (FRL, V0PLL at 593.407 MHz) plus
  DP 640x480@59.94Hz passes the check with DP on V0PLL / 2, and the
  commit then runs DP from AUPLL at 25.369 MHz (+7700 ppm), and the
  same happens for every mode the check accepted from the stale rate.

- A port that keeps running from the PLL is only seen through the
  clk_rate_exclusive_get() it holds, so whether another port gets the
  PLL depends on which of them the check looks at first, and a state
  is accepted or refused depending on the previous one.

Plan the PLL for all ports at once instead. The first port with a
modeset adds the other ports to the state, collects what each needs
(the DCLK, the closest CRU rate and whether it comes from the PLL's
current rate, the PLL rates the DCLK divides exactly from; for an
unchanged active port the PLL rate it runs from) and tries the current
PLL rate and then the rates the ports ask for, in port order, until one
serves every port: exactly for HDMI, within the pixel clock tolerance
for the other outputs, including from the planned PLL rate itself. Each
port's CRTC state records the PLL rate it runs from, which
atomic_enable programs and which an unchanged port keeps in the next
state, so the plan only depends on the new state and the current clock
rates, and a test-only commit and the real one agree. The CRU can only
divide by up to 32 on dclk_vop1/2, so a shared PLL rate must be at most
32 times the DCLK.

When a port's closest CRU rate comes from the PLL's current rate, what
the other CRU parents give it is hidden behind that rate. Rounding the
edges of the tolerance window reveals a close rate of another parent:
with that, the port is accepted with the PLL at any planned rate, and the
PLL is set to the new rate before the port's DCLK, so that the CRU does
not pick the old one. Otherwise it is only accepted with the PLL at its
current rate.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
